From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 05:41:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Name the sender of a deleted group message

When several members of a group send a message in the same second
and the messages are deleted, each deletion was reported in the same
words: "Message from 04:44:12 has been deleted". Seen on a Pixel 3a,
2026-11-07: three spam accounts posted at 04:44:12 and were removed,
and the chat got three identical notices.

Keep the sender's name with a group message as it is shown, carry it
through an edit, and name it in the notice: "The message <name> sent
at 04:44:12 has been deleted". Private chats, which have one sender,
and unsigned channel posts keep the old notice.

updateDeleteMessages does not say who deleted a message, but in a
supergroup or channel where we are an administrator the log of events
does. There the notice also names who deleted it: "X deleted their
message sent at ...", or "X deleted the message Y sent at ...", whether
they sent it being told by their ids. The log is waited for five seconds
at most; without it the notice is worded as above.

This is the pull request as it stands after review, squashed; it takes in:
- 9edebfd "Name who deleted a message too, where the log of events tells" (Mikhail T.)
- 5da70dd "Tell who deleted a message by who they are, and do not wait for the log" (Herman van Hazendonk)

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Submitted [https://github.com/UnitedMarsupials/tdlib-purple/pull/34]
